1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,100
One bad step.

2
00:00:01,260 --> 00:00:02,040
A crack.

3
00:00:02,510 --> 00:00:03,860
Your leg will not hold you.

4
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:09,730
Now put that moment thirty thousand years in
the past. No ambulance. No hospital.

5
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:11,910
And the sun is going down.

6
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:14,860
Game over? Not necessarily.

7
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:20,270
Archaeologists have found a person who
survived a devastating leg injury for years.

8
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:24,440
Some researchers think the damaged limb was
deliberately amputated.

9
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:29,820
We will get to that remarkable case. First,
our imaginary traveller has a more

10
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:31,090
immediate problem.

11
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:36,840
The shelter is over there. He is over here.
And the space between them has suddenly

12
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:37,910
become enormous.

13
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:43,790
He checks the leg again. Still broken. This is
going to complicate the afternoon.

14
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,830
A few minutes ago, that slope was a shortcut.
Now it is a wall.

15
00:00:49,090 --> 00:00:54,420
The stream was somewhere to drink. Now it is
an obstacle. Nothing in the landscape

16
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:55,390
has changed.

17
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:57,890
Your ability to cross it has.

18
00:00:58,310 --> 00:01:00,330
Our traveller is fictional.

19
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:04,990
The problem is real: someone has to notice
that you are missing before they can

20
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:05,550
help.

21
00:01:06,070 --> 00:01:11,920
In our story, two companions come back. One
hears the shouting. The other sees the

22
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:13,240
abandoned basket.

23
00:01:13,750 --> 00:01:18,610
They are not arriving with a magic cure. They
are arriving with time, effort, and

24
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:19,970
another pair of hands.

25
00:01:20,530 --> 00:01:25,540
Eventually, our traveller makes it back to
shelter with help. The landscape has not

26
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:26,620
become easier.

27
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:29,540
He simply no longer has to cross it alone.

28
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:35,890
For one glorious moment, being back at camp
feels like the end of the problem. Then

29
00:01:35,890 --> 00:01:36,780
you look down.

30
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:38,720
The leg has come home with you.

31
00:01:39,167 --> 00:01:41,967
A broken leg is not one single injury.

32
00:01:42,237 --> 00:01:46,477
A relatively stable fracture and a badly
displaced break do not create the same

33
00:01:46,477 --> 00:01:47,237
problems.

34
00:01:47,747 --> 00:01:50,167
Damage to surrounding tissue matters too.

35
00:01:50,597 --> 00:01:53,737
If the skin is broken, germs can enter the
wound.

36
00:01:54,047 --> 00:01:58,397
Serious fractures can also threaten nearby
blood vessels and nerves.

37
00:01:58,907 --> 00:02:04,307
Without modern treatment, those complications
could make a bad situation much worse.

38
00:02:04,827 --> 00:02:08,457
And even an injury that heals does not heal
overnight.

39
00:02:09,067 --> 00:02:14,907
Today, the NHS describes six to twelve weeks
as a usual healing range for a broken

40
00:02:14,907 --> 00:02:16,977
leg, sometimes longer.

41
00:02:17,417 --> 00:02:21,877
That is a modern comparison, not an ancient
recovery schedule.

42
00:02:22,477 --> 00:02:27,237
Our traveller was hoping for one uncomfortable
evening. He is now looking at a much

43
00:02:27,237 --> 00:02:28,507
longer arrangement.

44
00:02:28,807 --> 00:02:32,957
Tomorrow he needs food. The next day he needs
food.

45
00:02:33,417 --> 00:02:37,547
His body has absolutely no interest in how
inconvenient this is.

46
00:02:38,077 --> 00:02:41,167
Which means the next threat might not look
dramatic at all.

47
00:02:41,637 --> 00:02:45,207
It might look like an empty place where your
share of the work used to be.

48
00:02:45,633 --> 00:02:50,783
Imagine the jobs that still need doing around
this camp. Gathering fuel. Finding

49
00:02:50,783 --> 00:02:54,483
food. Carrying supplies. Maintaining shelter.

50
00:02:55,013 --> 00:02:59,323
The injury does not cancel any of them. It
changes who can do them.

51
00:02:59,873 --> 00:03:04,163
Someone bringing you food is spending time
they could spend elsewhere. Someone

52
00:03:04,163 --> 00:03:06,643
staying nearby may miss another task.

53
00:03:07,133 --> 00:03:13,093
Helping an injured person can be an ongoing
commitment, not a single heroic gesture.

54
00:03:13,483 --> 00:03:16,413
But needing help does not make someone
useless.

55
00:03:16,813 --> 00:03:21,943
In our imaginary camp, the traveller can still
share what he knows, watch what is

56
00:03:21,943 --> 00:03:25,263
happening, or work with his hands when he is
able.

57
00:03:25,813 --> 00:03:27,343
His role can change.

58
00:03:27,803 --> 00:03:30,893
We should not pretend every ancient group
behaved alike.

59
00:03:31,463 --> 00:03:36,643
Researchers studying Neanderthals argue that
care for injured people could make an

60
00:03:36,643 --> 00:03:38,933
important contribution to survival.

61
00:03:39,543 --> 00:03:43,873
The evidence includes injuries that healed
while the person continued living.

62
00:03:44,343 --> 00:03:48,023
That is a stronger clue than any scene we
invent.

63
00:03:48,483 --> 00:03:52,793
So let us leave our traveller by the fire and
meet someone whose bones actually

64
00:03:52,793 --> 00:03:53,643
survived.

65
00:03:54,067 --> 00:03:59,347
At Shanidar Cave, in what is now Iraq,
archaeologists found the remains of a

66
00:03:59,347 --> 00:04:01,837
Neanderthal known as Shanidar One.

67
00:04:02,317 --> 00:04:06,947
His case takes us beyond an imagined accident
and into a body that carried its

68
00:04:06,947 --> 00:04:08,437
injuries for years.

69
00:04:08,967 --> 00:04:11,817
His skeleton records several serious injuries.

70
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:17,407
Researchers describe damage around the left
eye, a severely impaired right arm, and

71
00:04:17,407 --> 00:04:19,347
a healed fracture in the right foot.

72
00:04:19,837 --> 00:04:25,007
Yet he lived into adulthood, with age at death
estimated around thirty-five to

73
00:04:25,007 --> 00:04:25,947
forty-five.

74
00:04:26,487 --> 00:04:31,007
That does not tell us every treatment he
received. It does not let us name the

75
00:04:31,007 --> 00:04:32,077
people who helped.

76
00:04:32,487 --> 00:04:36,587
It does show that the injuries were not all
immediate death sentences.

77
00:04:37,177 --> 00:04:42,887
His survival is widely interpreted as evidence
of support from others. The skeleton

78
00:04:42,887 --> 00:04:45,047
gives us injuries and healing.

79
00:04:45,457 --> 00:04:49,127
The daily work of care has to be reconstructed
more cautiously.

80
00:04:49,587 --> 00:04:54,787
Still, the cartoon of prehistory where
everyone simply abandons the injured is

81
00:04:54,787 --> 00:04:56,337
already looking shaky.

82
00:04:56,847 --> 00:05:01,827
And another discovery takes the question
further. What if part of a leg was gone?

83
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:08,603
In Liang Tebo cave, on the island of Borneo,
researchers uncovered a burial about

84
00:05:08,603 --> 00:05:10,493
thirty-one thousand years old.

85
00:05:10,933 --> 00:05:15,963
The young adult inside was missing the left
foot and part of the lower left leg.

86
00:05:16,363 --> 00:05:21,883
Missing bones alone would not prove surgery.
Bodies can be disturbed after burial.

87
00:05:22,353 --> 00:05:27,393
Here, the researchers examined something more
revealing: changes in the surviving

88
00:05:27,393 --> 00:05:28,353
leg bones.

89
00:05:28,903 --> 00:05:33,483
They identified extensive healing and argued
that the person had lived for at least

90
00:05:33,483 --> 00:05:35,113
another six to nine years

91
00:05:35,113 --> 00:05:38,733
after the injury, which probably happened in
childhood.

92
00:05:39,173 --> 00:05:44,063
Their interpretation, published in Nature in
twenty twenty-two, was deliberate

93
00:05:44,063 --> 00:05:45,663
surgical amputation.

94
00:05:46,113 --> 00:05:51,183
If correct, that places a successful operation
astonishingly far back in time.

95
00:05:51,893 --> 00:05:57,213
But the bones do not preserve the operator's
name, the instrument, or a recipe for

96
00:05:57,213 --> 00:05:58,353
managing pain.

97
00:05:59,103 --> 00:06:04,933
And there is a twist the headline often leaves
out: other specialists challenged the

98
00:06:04,933 --> 00:06:06,013
diagnosis.

99
00:06:06,433 --> 00:06:11,573
In a published response, orthopaedic surgeons
proposed a different explanation

100
00:06:11,723 --> 00:06:14,513
involving a severe fracture during growth.

101
00:06:14,983 --> 00:06:19,283
They argued that the skeletal changes did not
require a deliberate operation.

102
00:06:19,823 --> 00:06:22,833
The original researchers defended their
conclusion.

103
00:06:23,173 --> 00:06:27,723
They said the location and form of the damage
did not match that proposed fracture,

104
00:06:28,123 --> 00:06:31,713
and maintained that amputation best explained
what they observed.

105
00:06:32,253 --> 00:06:37,643
Here is the difficult part. A bone can show
that an injury healed.

106
00:06:38,033 --> 00:06:43,533
Reconstructing exactly what caused it is
another question. Surviving a terrible

107
00:06:43,533 --> 00:06:46,653
injury and proving an operation are different
claims.

108
00:06:47,023 --> 00:06:52,973
We also cannot fill the missing details with a
magical leaf, a miracle ointment, or

109
00:06:52,973 --> 00:06:55,113
an exact survival percentage.

110
00:06:55,763 --> 00:07:00,053
A dramatic possibility is still a possibility
that has to answer to the bones.

111
00:07:00,843 --> 00:07:05,733
And none of this makes a broken leg safe. It
gives us a more interesting answer than

112
00:07:05,733 --> 00:07:06,943
instant doom.

113
00:07:07,723 --> 00:07:12,843
Injury, survival, skill, and help could exist
in the same world.

114
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:19,050
Back at our imaginary camp, there is no
guarantee of a happy ending. A severe injury

115
00:07:19,050 --> 00:07:19,970
could kill.

116
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:25,760
An injury could heal badly. Someone might
survive with lasting pain or a different

117
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:26,600
way of moving.

118
00:07:27,220 --> 00:07:32,390
But look at what changed after that first
crack. Other people found him. Shelter

119
00:07:32,390 --> 00:07:35,180
became reachable. Food kept arriving.

120
00:07:35,690 --> 00:07:40,860
The world did not become gentle. He stopped
facing every part of it alone.

121
00:07:41,420 --> 00:07:46,310
Would a broken leg end your life thirty
thousand years ago? It could.

122
00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:50,240
But healed injuries show that it did not
always do so.

123
00:07:50,690 --> 00:07:54,450
The outcome depended on the injury and the
circumstances around it.

124
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:59,810
Our traveller notices a companion bringing
another meal. At the start, we were

125
00:07:59,810 --> 00:08:01,020
staring at his leg.

126
00:08:01,470 --> 00:08:05,190
By the end, we should also be looking at the
person who came back.

127
00:08:05,730 --> 00:08:11,480
Then he points toward the path and offers a
helpful suggestion. Apparently, there is

128
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:12,400
a shortcut.

129
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:17,320
His companion looks at him. He decides not to
finish that sentence.
